# Environments
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Data & outputs (never committed — corpora are licensed/restricted)
/data/

# summary/ is SCRIPT OUTPUT, all of it. Every leaderboard, report and parquet in
# there is rewritten by the next rescore, so tracking it meant a routine
# `rescore_all.sh` produced eighty modified files that said nothing about intent.
#
# What gets PUBLISHED is a deliberate act instead: evals/publish_records.py
# writes a dated snapshot to records/aligners/en/<YYYYMM>/ and repoints `latest`.
# A reader lands on something fixed, and a citation can name the month.
/summary/
/scratch/
*.wav
*.flac
*.parquet
*.TextGrid

# Caches
.cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Allow small test fixtures (tiny synthetic files) to be tracked
!fabench/**/test/fixtures/**

# Machine-specific staged-data config (real corpus paths). configs/ itself is
# gone -- roots live in .fabench.env now -- but the rule stays so an old
# checkout's file, or a hand-made variant put there out of habit, cannot be
# committed with a licensed path in it.
configs/

# per-aligner checkouts + venvs (large, rebuildable from evals/*/download_and_install.sh)
#
# ** rather than a fixed depth: a nested recipe puts its environment deeper
# (evals/aligners/mfa/v2.0.6/repo/ is four levels, not three), and the fixed
# pattern left 5 GB of micromamba env untracked-but-committable -- one
# `git add -A` from entering the history.
evals/**/repo/
evals/**/venv/

# Run artefacts. Logs and per-cell configs live WITH their tool
# (evals/<kind>/<tool>/{log,configs}/, including nested recipes at
# <tool>/exps/<name>/ and <tool>/v<version>/); evals/log/ and evals/configs/
# hold the cross-tool orchestration logs and scoring configs, which span every
# tool by definition. Every one is regenerated from evals/<kind>/<tool>/
# config.yaml by the next run, so none is worth tracking.
#
evals/*/*/log/
evals/**/log/
evals/log/
evals/**/configs/

# Generated per-cell configs, which now sit IN the cell as config.yaml. They
# carry absolute machine paths INCLUDING the staged licensed corpus root, so
# this must fail CLOSED: ignore every config.yaml under evals/, then re-admit
# exactly the tracked ones. That allow-list mirrors fabench/paths.py::
# _TOOL_GLOBS, which is the definition of "a recipe" -- if a new recipe shape
# is added there, it needs a line here too.
evals/**/config.yaml
!/evals/config.yaml
!/evals/*/*/config.yaml
!/evals/*/*/exps/*/config.yaml
!/evals/*/*/v*/config.yaml

# setuptools build artefacts (a stale one silently poisoned a wheel check)
build/
*.egg-info/

# path.sh drops these in $PWD; make_noisy.py cds first, but be safe
/steps

# Gold references materialised by dataprep/datasets/export_refs.py.
# These are TIMIT .PHN / Buckeye .phones reformatted -- the LICENSED
# annotation itself. LDC and OSU both forbid redistribution, so the
# files live in-repo for locality but must never enter the history.
#
# ** rather than a fixed depth. The pattern was datasets/*/*/split/, which
# matched datasets/en/timit/split/ exactly; moving the tree to
# datasets/languages/en/timit/split/ added one level and the rule stopped
# matching, leaving 14 MB of licensed transcripts stageable with no warning.
# A depth-counted ignore fails OPEN, which is the wrong direction for this
# particular file.
datasets/**/split/*.ref.jsonl
datasets/**/split/*.ref.json

# mkdocs local build output
/site/

# Aligner hypothesis outputs embed the licensed corpora's transcripts
# (word labels = the TIMIT/Buckeye text; LDC and OSU forbid redistribution).
# Same rule as the gold refs above: local artifacts, never in history.
hyp/
hyp.jsonl

# Logs and server-ops artifacts are machine-local, never tracked. The olign
# server scripts operate a proprietary binary that is not part of this repo
# (the public posture is interface-only: the adapter + a reachable endpoint).
install.log
*.pid
fabench/dataprep/noisemix/*.log
evals/aligners/olign/run/
evals/aligners/olign/start_server.sh
evals/aligners/olign/stop_server.sh

# A tool's entire per-language output tree:
#   evals/<kind>/<tool>/<lang>/<corpus>/<subset>/<condition>/
# ALL of it is derived -- the config generated from the recipe, the alignment
# from a run, the scores from that alignment in seconds -- and hyp.jsonl carries
# the licensed transcripts besides. One rule for the subtree, so anything a
# future stage drops in a cell is covered without a new pattern.
#
# One line per language (`en` is the only one today) rather than a glob like
# [a-z][a-z]/, which would match far more by accident.
#
# POSITIONAL, not by name. `evals/**/en/` would have matched ANY directory
# called `en` under evals/ -- including the five gettext catalogues in the MFA
# conda envs (evals/aligners/mfa/repo/mamba/envs/mfa/share/locale/en and four
# siblings). Those are already excluded by evals/**/repo/ above, so the effect
# was invisible, but the rule would have reached a locale `en/` sitting outside
# a repo/ or venv/ tree. A tool's output can only appear at the three recipe
# shapes below, so name them: same list as fabench/paths.py::_TOOL_GLOBS, which
# is the definition of "a recipe" -- add a line here when one is added there.
evals/*/*/en/
evals/*/*/exps/*/en/
evals/*/*/v*/en/

# A cell's output: the alignment AND its scores, in one directory
#   evals/<kind>/<tool>/<lang>/<corpus>/<subset>/<condition>/
# All of it is derived -- hyp from a run, scores from that hyp in seconds --
# and hyp.jsonl carries the licensed transcripts besides.
#
# The .csv rule is the one that is easy to miss: these used to sit under
# evals/**/exp/, so moving them beside the hyp exposed 41 leaderboards that had
# been covered only by the old path. (*.parquet is already ignored globally.)
evals/**/*.csv
evals/**/exp/
# (evals/**/exp/ kept for older checkouts; not to be confused with
# <tool>/exps/, the tracked experiment CONFIGS.)

# Experiment configs under <tool>/exps/ are TRACKED on purpose: evals/ exists
# to record what was run with which parameters, and an ablation's config is
# exactly that record. Only its outputs (hyp/, log/, venv/) are ignored, by
# the rules above.
#
# EXCEPT a closed system's, where the record is not ours to publish. These
# configs differ from one another only in `name:` and a localhost port -- the
# variation is server-side, in a binary that is not in this repo -- so tracking
# them adds nothing a reader can reproduce, while the directory names disclose
# the tunable axes of a system whose design is undisclosed. The published row is
# the shipped configuration; the sweep belongs with the system it belongs to.
evals/aligners/olign/exps/

# This machine's settings (FABENCH_*). The tracked template is
# .fabench.env.example; the live file names real paths and is local.
.fabench.env

# Working research: plans, findings, and exploratory notes. Not part of the
# distribution -- the reviewed, published tier is the READMEs beside the code
# and the tables in summary/. Covers the whole tree, including the analysis
# scripts that moved into fabench/analyze/.
/research/